      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently cataloging a series of ceramic shards from a Late Helladic II stratum. I'm having some trouble distinguishing between different firing techniques used in this period. Could you explain how I can differentiate between oxidized and reduced firing atmospheres based on the clay body and surface color?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oxidized firing is a bit like a well-behaved, polite guest at a dinner party, while reduced firing is more like a mischievous, chaotic party where things get a little wilder and unpredictable. When you look at your Late Helladic II shards, you're essentially trying to tell the difference between a tidy room and a room where the furniture has been rearranged by a very small, very determined group of goblins.\n\nIn an **oxidized atmosphere**, the air is full of oxygen, which acts like a strict teacher correcting a student's posture. The iron in your clay turns into hematite, giving you that classic warm, reddish-brown or orange-buff color. It's a stable, predictable result, much like a well-organized library where every book is in its proper place. You might see a slightly matte surface, or perhaps a glossy glaze if they got lucky with a slip, but the overall vibe is one of controlled combustion.\n\nConversely, **reduced firing** is where the goblins really come out to play. By removing the oxygen, the iron in the clay gets reduced to magnetite, creating those deep, mysterious black, dark brown, or even greenish hues",
      "source": "target",
